1️⃣ Why do we need an amplifier at all in a resistor DAC?
When you build a weighted-resistor DAC (or even an R–2R ladder), the binary resistors feed into a summing point.
Without an op amp:

The output voltage would depend on the load connected to it — connecting any device would change the voltage.

The resistors need a virtual ground point to produce correct current sums. Without that, the binary weighting gets distorted.

You’d have low output current capability — your circuit might not be able to drive the next stage.

An op amp in a summing amplifier configuration:

Keeps the summing node at (virtual) ground — binary weighting stays accurate.

Provides high input impedance for the DAC resistors and low output impedance for driving loads.

Lets you set the gain easily so your output range matches your desired Vfs.

2️⃣ Why specifically an op amp and not another amplifier?
We use an op amp because:

It can handle DC and low-frequency analog signals accurately (audio to kHz range — perfect for DAC outputs).

It has very high input impedance (doesn’t disturb DAC resistor network).

It can sum multiple inputs naturally — other amplifiers (like BJT or MOSFET amps) are good for power gain but bad for precise summing.

It gives very high gain so feedback can make the behavior predictable (linear, accurate).

It can operate single-supply if needed (handy for microcontroller DAC circuits).

3️⃣ What type of op amp should you use for a DAC?
It depends on what you care about most:

Requirement	Recommended feature in op amp
Precision & low offset	Low-offset voltage op amp (e.g., OP07, OPA277)
High speed (fast DAC)	High slew rate & bandwidth (e.g., OPA627, LM318)
Low power	Low-power CMOS op amp (e.g., MCP6002, TLV2372)
Cheap & general use	LM358, TL072 (good for learning, not super precise)

For ESP32 + DAC learning, you can use:

LM358 → cheap, works at 3.3–5V, OK for slow waveforms (like staircases).

TL072 → better for audio frequency, cleaner.

